We now turn our attention to theoretical guarantees for the RLHF-Sharpening algorithm family, which uses tools from reinforcement learning to optimize the self-reward function. When specialized to maximum-likelihood sharpening, the RL objective used by RLHF-Sharpening takes the form

\displaystyle\widehat{\pi}\approx\argmax_{\pi\in\Pi}\left\{\En_{\pi}\left[\log\pi_{\texttt{base}}(y\mid{}x)\right]-\beta D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{base}}\right)\right\}

for $\beta>0$. The exact optimizer $\pi^{\star}_{\beta}=\argmax_{\pi\in\Pi}\left\{\En_{\pi}\left[\log\pi_{\texttt{base}}(y\mid{}x)\right]-\beta D_{\mathsf{KL}}\left(\pi\,\|\,\pi_{\texttt{base}}\right)\right\}$ for this objective has the form $\pi^{\star}_{\beta}(y\mid{}x)\propto\pi_{\texttt{base}}^{1+\beta^{-1}}(y\mid{}x)$, which converges to a sharpened model (per Section 3.1) as $\beta\to{}0$.

